# Normalize line endings for all text files regardless of any contributor's or
# environment's core.autocrlf setting. Without this, whether a file is stored
# as LF or CRLF depends on whoever last touched it — a real source of drift
# across a Windows dev, a Linux CI/VM, and the Cowork sandbox bridge, all
# writing to the same repo. text=auto lets git still skip binaries.
#
# Order matters: later rules win. This line MUST stay above the *.sh and *.bat
# rules below — moved under them it would force .bat to LF and silently break
# every Windows batch script. Nothing about that failure is loud, so it is
# guarded by packages/engine/mcp-server/tests/packaging/gitattributes.test.ts.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for scanned evidence documents: text=auto samples only the
# first 8000 bytes for a NUL, and an uncompressed or ASCII-heavy scan can slip
# past that and get mangled. eval/tests/e2e/*/provided-documents/ is a growing
# pile of exactly that shape.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF endings on every platform.
#
# Git for Windows defaults core.autocrlf=true, so without this these files check
# out with CRLF on a Windows clone — and a `#!/bin/sh` script with CRLF does not
# run under Git Bash. The trailing \r becomes part of the last token on every
# line: `set -eu` parses as `set -eu<CR>` ("invalid option", exit 2), `exit 0` as
# `exit $'0\r'` ("numeric argument required", exit 255).
#
# Not hypothetical. The post-checkout hook runs scripts/link-worktree.sh on
# `git worktree add`, and calls it with `|| true` so as never to fail a
# checkout — meaning on Windows it fails *silently*, leaving the new worktree
# with no linked node_modules or .env and no clue why.
#
# Extensionless scripts (git hooks) have no rule of their own — they had none
# at all before the blanket `* text=auto eol=lf` above, which is what covers
# them now.
*.sh text eol=lf

*.bat text eol=crlf
